<channel><title><![CDATA[Alan Cavagnaro - Blog]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog]]></link><description><![CDATA[Blog]]></description><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 06:08:04 -0500</pubDate><generator>Weebly</generator><item><title><![CDATA[What the World Series Reveals About How We Build Our Cities and Communities (October 2025 Repost)]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/what-the-world-series-reveals-about-how-we-build-our-cities-and-communities-october-2025-repost]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/what-the-world-series-reveals-about-how-we-build-our-cities-and-communities-october-2025-repost#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Thu, 11 Dec 2025 18:22:05 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/what-the-world-series-reveals-about-how-we-build-our-cities-and-communities-october-2025-repost</guid><description><![CDATA[    View: Left - Rogers Centre, photo by The Land of Dustin. Right - Dodger Stadium, photo via Pixabay.   &#8203;I love baseball. It is America&rsquo;s greatest pastime. Tonight is Game Two of the World Series, and if last night was any indication, we&rsquo;re in for a treat. If you are one of the lucky fans to have a ticket to a World Series game, you will encounter a different atmosphere, depending on which stadium you attend. I am not referring to the fan bases, but rather to what each ballpa [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/2d324276-9d7b-4e9d-bf46-b508c358a399-1789x1014_orig.webp" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">View: Left - Rogers Centre, photo by The Land of Dustin. Right - Dodger Stadium, photo via Pixabay.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br />&#8203;I love baseball. It is America&rsquo;s greatest pastime. Tonight is Game Two of the World Series, and if last night was any indication, we&rsquo;re in for a treat. If you are one of the lucky fans to have a ticket to a World Series game, you will encounter a different atmosphere, depending on which stadium you attend. I am not referring to the fan bases, but rather to what each ballpark and its surrounding area offer to the community.<br />&#8203;<br />Before and after the game tonight from the Rogers Centre (home of the Toronto Blue Jays), you can visit attractions such as the CN Tower, Ripley&rsquo;s Aquarium, the countless amount of bars, or even HTO Park on the Inner Harbour, all within a five-minute walk from where game two will be played, as well as the robust and reliable Toronto Subway.<br /><br /><span>The Blue Jays face the Los Angeles Dodgers, the&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/mlb/2025/04/02/2025-mlb-team-payrolls-highest-lowest/82751012007/">second-highest spending team in the MLB</a><span>. That fact is important when comparing the two communities these teams represent and play in.<br /><br />The Dodgers were previously located in Brooklyn, New York. They left in the mid-twentieth century for greater pastures in Los Angeles, chasing a new stadium and market. Los Angeles now ranks as the&nbsp;</span><a href="https://creators.yahoo.com/lifestyle/story/10-of-the-best-sports-cities-in-the-us-ranked-162844696.html">best sports market in the country</a><span>, with Dodger Stadium as the highest capacity stadium in the league at 56,000 seats.<br /></span><br />Yet, for all its size and history, Dodger Stadium stands largely isolated from the city it represents. Nestled in the hills of Chavez Ravine, it is surrounded by sprawling parking lots, a stark reminder of the communities that once lived there.<br /><br /></div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a href='https://laist.com/news/la-history/dodger-stadium-chavez-ravine-battle' target='_blank'> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/1a0f5a97-d7fe-49bb-91fd-fbc3f98a5c3d-1460x1178_orig.webp" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">July 20, 1953: &ldquo;Home owners from Chavez Ravine, Rose Hills, and Pacoima, tell Mayor Norris Poulson (left) to fight on for abandonment of housing projects.&rdquo; Credit: (Herald-Examiner Collection/Los Angeles Public Library Collection, LAist).</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span>The stadium displaced the Mexican-American communities of La Loma, Palo Verde, and Bishop, forcing&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/news/latino/dodgers-stadium-reparations-families-land-california-angeles-rcna145152">approximately 1,800 families to relocate</a><span>. They were told the land would soon host a new public housing project and that they could return when it was complete. That project never broke ground. Less than a decade later, the Dodgers arrived, and the empty land, which was once filled with homes, schools, and small businesses, was handed over for the construction of a stadium. The fight over that land became known as the&nbsp;</span><em><a href="https://laist.com/news/la-history/dodger-stadium-chavez-ravine-battle">Battle of Chavez Ravine</a></em><span>.<br /></span><br />Today, the Chavez Ravine community is unrecognizable. What once was a thriving neighborhood has been replaced by asphalt and concrete, surrounded by miles of empty parking lots that sit unused most days of the year. The area generates little local activity, offers few public amenities, and creates barriers instead of connections.<br /><br />That&rsquo;s the problem we find ourselves in today. The current model treats stadiums as destinations, not as parts of a living city, and in doing so, it leaves enormous economic and social potential untapped.<br />&#8203;<br />On the other side of the continent sits the City of Toronto in Canada, a community that shows up for its Blue Jays. Despite not being the most marketable city for sports or the highest spender of teams for baseball, the Blue Jays consistently punch above their weight, not only in on-the-field performance, but for creating a sense of community in downtown Toronto.</div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/5412f1e5-75fb-439f-90eb-efc522449694-2048x2048_orig.webp" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">View: Top - Toronto skyline, 2018. Bottom - Toronto skyline, 1990.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span>The last time the Toronto Blue Jays made the World Series in 1993, the city&rsquo;s population sat at just shy of 4.1 million. Today, that number is nearly&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.macrotrends.net/global-metrics/cities/20402/toronto/population">6.5 million</a><span>. That is a 58.54% increase in population for Toronto. The Rogers Centre and Blue Jays baseball isn&rsquo;t the sole reason why Toronto saw a surge in development over the past few decades, but rather, it suggests a pattern in smart growth planning from city officials.<br /><br /></span>Rogers Centre sits where old railyards used to be a half-century ago. That allowed prime development space for not only a new baseball stadium, but also restaurants, offices, bars, parks, and anything in between. With optimal proximity to public transportation (train, subway, and bus), the area saw demand for development in the early 2000s.<br /><span></span></div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a href='https://alextabascio.wordpress.com/2019/06/12/torontos-zoning-by-law-diversity/' target='_blank'> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/4e1c3dc9-27ed-4487-b71d-7402bed3155a-2048x1448_orig.webp" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">Figure: Zoning map of the City of Toronto (circa Alex Tabascio GIS).</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><span><br />It also helps that the area surrounding the Rogers Centre is zoned for mixed-use, allowing for more than one land use type on a plot of land. As a result, downtown Toronto became a community. In nature, it was a&nbsp;</span><a href="https://esl.uchicago.edu/2023/11/01/third-places-what-are-they-and-why-are-they-important-to-american-culture/">third place</a><span>&nbsp;for most residents who either lived or worked in the area. This is what happens when a ballpark or stadium is planned for the future.</span><br /><br />The City of Los Angeles, like many American cities in the mid-to-late 20th century, embraced a model of stadium building that prioritized cars over communities. From the 1960s through the 1980s, cities across the country built &ldquo;cookie-cutter&rdquo; multi-use stadiums. These were massive circular structures meant to host football, baseball, and concerts alike. While Dodger Stadium never adopted this model, it followed suit with many cities in prioritizing car-centric development for its stadiums. The nearest public transit stop is Chinatown Station, a 31-minute walk through endless parking from the stadium.<br /><br />They were efficient, practical, and brutally identical. Whether you were in Cincinnati, Pittsburgh, or Philadelphia, you would find nearly the same concrete bowl surrounded by endless parking, not even realizing which city you were in. Not thinking about the fan experience or the sense of community outside the game. That era has passed. Today&rsquo;s ballparks are no longer one-size-fits-all. They&rsquo;re increasingly designed to capture the fan experience: open concourses, local food vendors, team-specific architecture, and views that tie the game to the skyline.<br /><br />Now, many of these cookie-cutter stadiums no longer exist except for one, the Rogers Centre. It stands as a prime example of how to integrate a bad stadium concept into a community-oriented solution.<br /><br />We&rsquo;ve learned how to design for fans &mdash; now, we must learn to design for communities. Fortunately enough, that future isn&rsquo;t theoretical, but it&rsquo;s already emerging.<span><br /><br />In Minneapolis, U.S. Bank Stadium connects directly to light rail and spurred a wave of nearby development that has revitalized the city&rsquo;s East Town neighborhood. In suburban Atlanta, the Braves&rsquo; Truist Park redefined stadium planning entirely, integrating its urban core of commercial shops, bars, and restaurants within the stadium itself, in an area the city calls &ldquo;The Battery Atlanta.&rdquo; These are state-of-the-art stadiums built within the past ten years with new visions than their predecessors. The goal was fan experience on&nbsp;</span><em>and</em><span>&nbsp;off the field.</span><br /><br />Believe it or not, this stadium concept is not new. It vanished when cars hit the road, and the stadium experience off the field was deprioritized. Look at classic ballparks like Fenway Park in Boston, or Wrigley Field in Chicago. Both are at least a centurion old and still have some of the greatest fan experiences beyond the outfield walls. That&rsquo;s the thing, we lost our footing a long time ago with new stadiums. We forgot about the city and community these teams are supposed to represent and support.<span><br /><br />Stadiums, when done correctly, are powerful economic engines. They generate thousands of construction and long-term hospitality jobs, boost local spending on game days and beyond, and strengthen the local tax base through new development around the stadium. For example, a recent MIT and Northwestern University study found that a median sports facility yields roughly&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166046222000916?">$11.3 million in additional spending for local food, accommodation, and retail businesses</a><span>.</span><br /><br />That is serious economic growth, and yet, much of that potential is wasted when seas of asphalt parking lots surround a sports venue. This kind of stadium design is the difference between a family taking the subway in for the morning to visit shops, eat lunch, then hit an afternoon game, compared to one that will drive in and take no time to explore the community beyond the gates.<br /><br />As we watch the World Series unfold, it&rsquo;s worth remembering that stadiums are more than symbols of competition. They are reflections of our urban values. We can choose to build car-centric stadiums and communities, ones that negate the fan experience. Or, we can create a place where people want to be, utilizing the local economy. The next era of stadium design won&rsquo;t just determine how we experience sports, but rather, it will shape how we experience community itself.<br /><span></span></div>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[We Are Running Out of Time to Meet Our SDGs]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/we-are-running-out-of-time-to-meet-our-sdgs]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/we-are-running-out-of-time-to-meet-our-sdgs#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Thu, 09 Oct 2025 22:21:46 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/we-are-running-out-of-time-to-meet-our-sdgs</guid><description><![CDATA[Reflections on what I learned attending the 2025 UN Global Goals Week.      Photo courtesy of Ricoh USA, Global SDG Action Month.   For one week, New York City transitions from the world&rsquo;s crossroads into a stage for our collective ambition. Roads are blocked off, protests exist on every street corner, and your bus may be delayed, like it was for me. But that is for very good reason. Each September, New York City transforms into the capital of the world. Diplomats, CEOs, advocates, student [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="paragraph"><span><span style="color:rgb(102, 102, 102)">Reflections on what I learned attending the 2025 UN Global Goals Week.</span></span><br /><span></span></div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/og-about-us-social-global-sdgs_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">Photo courtesy of Ricoh USA, Global SDG Action Month.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span><span>For one week, New York City transitions from the world&rsquo;s crossroads into a stage for our collective ambition. Roads are blocked off, protests exist on every street corner, and your bus may be delayed, like it was for me. But that is for very good reason. Each September, New York City transforms into the capital of the world. Diplomats, CEOs, advocates, students, and observers watch on for our world's most prominent week. Climate Week, the UN Global Goals Week, and the United Nations General Assembly unfold side by side, elevating the city into the epicenter of global impact.</span></span><span><span><br /><br />Global Goals Week is the chance to shed light on the United Nations&rsquo; </span><a href="https://sdgs.un.org/goals"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">Sustainable Development Goals</span></a><span>, also known as SDGs. They were enacted in 2015 as a universal call to action that set 17 ambitious goals to guide nations toward a fairer and more sustainable world by 2030. These goals represent our future, and what that future may look like depends on the willingness of leaders across the globe to act.</span></span><span><span><br /><br />For 2025, this was my first time attending, primarily for Global Goals Week, now serving as a UNA-USA Global Goals Ambassador for </span><a href="https://sdgs.un.org/goals/goal11"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">SDG 11, Sustainable Cities and Communities</span></a><span>. At its core, the 11th goal in the SDGs challenges us to build communities where affordability, accessibility, and sustainability coexist. That looks like expanding public transportation, incentivizing the construction of affordable homes, or even planning more road diets to expand protected bike lanes.</span></span><span><span><br /><br />On paper, this week is meant to advance the UN&rsquo;s SDGs, which provide a blueprint for a sustainable tomorrow. Yet in practice, though, they serve more as a test of our collective commitment to them. Can our shared ideals stand up to the realities of action? We all want change, but how?<br /><br /></span></span><span><span>For those who don't know me well, you may be asking why I attended in the first place. Believe it or not, the parallel between global action and our locally built urban environments is closer than you think.</span></span><br /><span></span></div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/20250925-160141_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">Panel on &ldquo;Sustainability as a Business Imperative&rdquo; at UN Global Goals Week 2025 in New York City.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span><span>During UN Global Goals Week 2025, I attended </span><a href="https://luma.com/niki6y64"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">The Business Imperative for Sustainability</span></a><span>, an in-person panel featuring leaders from finance, fashion, and consumer products. Speakers discussed why sustainability is not just good for ethics but also beneficial for the private sector and business growth. Panelists explained that environmental and sustainable strategies are central to growth.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The relevance of SDG 11 to me was clear. Private entities are involved in the conversation because top-down mandates from the public sector do not always work. Buy-in from both small and large businesses toward greener initiatives will enable future communities to increase the utilization of sustainable infrastructure. This could look like electric buses, light rail, hybrid technology, and even bicycles made from recycled materials.&nbsp;</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The private sector can help shape cities by investing in important goals and driving innovations that improve how people live and how our communities can grow.</span><span> However, lasting progress also depends on strong public-sector leadership. Communities need to make an active effort to achieve sustainable development.&nbsp;</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>But sitting in those discussions also raised difficult questions. Who benefits when sustainability becomes a business imperative? Do these global partnerships truly reach the communities most vulnerable to climate change, or do they reinforce existing divides? If clean energy and sustainable housing remain out of reach for working families, then we are only greening inequality, not solving it. Take a city that rolls out electric scooters through a private partnership but limits them to its wealthier districts. The people who might rely on them most are effectively excluded.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>When I attended COP29 last fall in Azerbaijan, there was a lasting quote that stuck with me for global finance. I sat in on the plenary room for the second week when I heard </span><a href="https://www.fbcnews.com.fj/news/calls-for-urgent-action-at-cop29/"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">Attorney General Graham Leung of Fiji</span></a><span> deliver a passionate address, </span><span>&ldquo;On finance ambition, let me emphasize again that an ambitious outcome must focus not only on how much but also on how the finance will flow.&rdquo;</span><span> That quote is often what I think about when we have these global discussions. Oftentimes, you cannot throw money at your problems if you have no clue how it will be utilized. The same applies here for our SDGs. We all want sustainability, but sustainability for whom?</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>One of the biggest obstacles to achieving the SDGs is money, or rather, a lack thereof. The world needs </span><a href="https://unctad.org/sdg-costing/about"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">$5.4 to $6.4 trillion per year from 2023 through 2030</span></a><span> just to stay on track across key SDGs. For developing countries, that means a financing gap of about </span><a href="https://unstats.un.org/sdgs/report/2025/"><span style="color:rgb(0, 125, 20); font-weight:400">$4 trillion annually</span></a><span> in sectors like infrastructure, health, education, water &amp; sanitation, climate mitigation/adaptation, and food security. We currently run the risk of not meeting our goals for 2030.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The gap has only widened since the pandemic. Debt burdens rise and borrowing costs increase universally. Meanwhile, global investments are growing unevenly. Some sectors, such as renewable energy and health, are seeing gains, but other areas, like water, sanitation, and food systems, struggle to attract sufficient international financing.&nbsp;</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>This is a problem. Yet moments like Global Goals Week show the other side of the equation. The collaboration, potential, and creativity that emerge when governments, NGOs, advocates, and private actors cross paths and work together.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The beauty of Global Goals Week lies in its ambition. It creates an ecosystem of ideas and possible paths forward. But its challenge is the same as the UN&rsquo;s broader one: bridging the gap between commitment and capacity</span><span>. The idea of SDGs are not unreachable or abstract targets. It very much deals with local communities and how to help. It could look like local infrastructure investment or even more affordable homes. Without grounding the global in the local, progress risks becoming performative. All talk, no action.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Walking through New York City just a few weeks ago reminded me of how far we have come since the SDGs were adopted in 2015. They serve as the catalyst for how much further we have to go and what is left to do. We do not need bigger promises, but rather, better follow-through.&nbsp;</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The banners and speeches matter. But the measure of success will be what happens after everyone leaves New York. Will the private sector fulfill its promises? Will we deliver on the goals, or will we need a post-2030 agenda to plan for the next generation? Only time will tell. Sustainable development will not be achieved by the loudest voices, but by the quiet persistence of local communities everywhere, turning these goals into reality.</span></span></div>  <div><div style="height: 20px; overflow: hidden; width: 100%;"></div> <hr class="styled-hr" style="width:100%;"></hr> <div style="height: 20px; overflow: hidden; width: 100%;"></div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><em>To view future readings earlier and delivered to your mailbox, please consider subscribing to my Substack. It is free, and allows you to stay up to date with my writings - you can click the button below!</em></div>  <div style="text-align:center;"><div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div> <a class="wsite-button wsite-button-small wsite-button-highlight" href="https://alancavagnaro.substack.com/" target="_blank"> <span class="wsite-button-inner">Subscribe to my substack!</span> </a> <div style="height: 10px; overflow: hidden;"></div></div>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Your Town’s Future Is Being Decided Without You. I Know, Because I'm in the Room.]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/your-towns-future-is-being-decided-without-you-i-know-because-im-in-the-room]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/your-towns-future-is-being-decided-without-you-i-know-because-im-in-the-room#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Tue, 22 Jul 2025 02:48:05 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/your-towns-future-is-being-decided-without-you-i-know-because-im-in-the-room</guid><description><![CDATA[&#8203;From outsider to elected official, I&rsquo;ve seen how power works, and who gets excluded.      Old Lyme residents showed up in force at the April 9th, 2025, zoning meeting to oppose new homes.   A new report from last week confirms what many of us already suspected: land-use boards across Connecticut are wildly unrepresentative of the communities they serve. And that has real consequences on our statewide housing stock.According to&nbsp;new research from the Housing Collective, a non-pro [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div class="paragraph">&#8203;From outsider to elected official, I&rsquo;ve seen how power works, and who gets excluded.</div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/old-lyme-zoning-4-9-25_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">Old Lyme residents showed up in force at the April 9th, 2025, zoning meeting to oppose new homes.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span style="color:rgb(42, 42, 42)">A new report from last week confirms what many of us already suspected: land-use boards across Connecticut are wildly unrepresentative of the communities they serve. And that has real consequences on our statewide housing stock.</span><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><span>According to&nbsp;</span><a href="https://cho.thehousingcollective.org/impact/participation-local-control-land-use-connecticut">new research from the Housing Collective</a><span>, a non-profit group based in Connecticut, over 90% of land-use board members in Fairfield and New London Counties are white. The majority are men. The median age is 60 to 65. Most are homeowners. Meanwhile, renters, younger people, and families of color, those most impacted by Connecticut&rsquo;s housing shortage, are essentially removed from the room where zoning decisions are made. They do not have a seat at the table, and that is a problem.</span><br /><br /><span>This is not just a numbers problem. It&rsquo;s a power problem. When land-use boards are dominated by people with similar backgrounds and life experiences, their decisions are shaped by a narrow set of interests, often to preserve the norm, block new housing, and keep neighborhoods the way they have always been. Exclusive, with no door open. How is the generation of tomorrow supposed to find a home when today&rsquo;s leaders control what is and is not available?</span></font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">Let&rsquo;s face it. There are structural reasons why wealthy, white, male individuals are often overrepresented on local zoning boards. These positions tend to attract those with the time, resources, and incentives to preserve the status quo, rather than opening the door.</font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">In contrast, working parents, like a single mother juggling multiple jobs, or a full-time college student, may not have the flexibility to attend lengthy meetings or run for local office. In communities where zoning board seats require election, the time and effort needed to campaign can further discourage participation. Why is it important for working class people to be involved in the discussion, too, you must ask?</font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">When policies are shared for the few, they do not reflect the needs of the many. In my hometown, I&rsquo;ve heard more than one person claim that &ldquo;only homeowners should serve on zoning.&rdquo; But here&rsquo;s the truth: renters live in our communities, too. So do students. So do seniors on fixed incomes, and families just trying to get by. Everyone who calls a place home deserves a voice in shaping it.</font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><span>We need more people at the table who understand what it&rsquo;s like to struggle to find a home, not just those focused on protecting what they already have.</span><br /><br /><span>As an elected land use commissioner from South Windsor, CT, it is imperative that the people who serve on these local boards represent the community as a whole as they shape the outcomes for entire communities. Broadening participation ensures that local decisions reflect the full range of community needs, notably when those decisions influence where people can live, raise a family, or settle down.</span></font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><a href="https://projects.iq.harvard.edu/sites/projects.iq.harvard.edu/files/pegroup/files/foster_warren_nimby_problem.pdf">Local zoning boards have historically played a key role in blocking new development</a><span>, especially multi-family housing, a practice often referred to as &ldquo;Not In My Backyard,&rdquo; or NIMBYism. Even former&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.cnn.com/2025/07/14/politics/obama-democrats-message">President Barack Obama has spoken out against this mindset</a><span>, calling attention to its shortcomings and the need for more inclusive, abundant housing policies.</span></font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><span>NIMBYs obstruct the political process and act in bad faith.&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.cato.org/commentary/never-underestimate-nimbyisms-impact-economic-growth">Our local economies hurt when new development is halted arbitrarily</a><span>. At that point, the local tax base thins, and we have one group to thank for that.</span></font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">Zoning boards with elected members, perhaps counterintuitively, are often more dominated by individuals primarily focused on protecting their own property values and neighborhoods. Appointed commissioners, however, are selected by town councils or political parties, usually bringing a broader perspective shaped by policy priorities or community-wide goals. It is up to you to break that mold and change the status quo.</font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">The good news? There&rsquo;s a real opportunity to act. Across states, such as Connecticut, countless amounts of land-use board seats are either vacant or expired, many quietly falling through the cracks in our communities. By actively recruiting from the full diversity of our communities, we help ensure these boards better reflect the people they serve.<br />&#8203;</font><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><span>You can also do what I did, and challenge the political establishment and run for your local land use board, if your community allows it.</span><br /><br /><span>So here&rsquo;s your homework: If we want a community that is reflective of us all, it starts with who is serving at the table. Consider getting involved with your local land-use board, local town committee, or encouraging someone you trust to do so, because if you&rsquo;re not at the table, someone else will be speaking on your behalf.</span></font><br /><br /><em><font color="#2a2a2a">The official and updated article can be found on Next City by clicking <a href="https://nextcity.org/urbanist-news/your-towns-future-is-being-decided-without-you.-i-know-because-im-in-the-ro" target="_blank">here.</a></font></em></div>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Closing This Chapter: I Will Not Be Seeking Re-Election]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/closing-this-chapter-i-will-not-be-seeking-re-election]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/closing-this-chapter-i-will-not-be-seeking-re-election#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Tue, 03 Jun 2025 21:50:46 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/closing-this-chapter-i-will-not-be-seeking-re-election</guid><description><![CDATA[         Hey, South Windsor!After thoughtful reflection, I have decided not to seek re-election to the Planning and Zoning Commission this year. I also do not intend to run for any other elected position at this time.When I first ran for office at 19, it was not because I had all the answers. It was because I saw people like me being priced out of South Windsor. I believed our town deserved leaders who understood that urgency. I wanted to be a voice for the next generation. Against the odds, we  [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/not-running-2025-post_orig.png" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%"></div> </div></div>  <div class="wsite-spacer" style="height:10px;"></div>  <div class="paragraph"><span>Hey, South Windsor!</span><br /><br /><span>After thoughtful reflection, I have decided not to seek re-election to the Planning and Zoning Commission this year. I also do not intend to run for any other elected position at this time.</span><br /><br /><span>When I first ran for office at 19, it was not because I had all the answers. It was because I saw people like me being priced out of South Windsor. I believed our town deserved leaders who understood that urgency. I wanted to be a voice for the next generation. Against the odds, we made history together. Since that day, it has been one of the greatest honors of my life to serve this wonderful community.</span><br /><br /><span>During my time on the Commission, we made real, lasting progress. We updated zoning to expand access to affordable housing. We prioritized clean energy, open space preservation, and land use policies that meet the urgency of climate change. We had hard conversations and made tough decisions, always working to build a stronger, more sustainable future. I am proud to have kept every promise I made when I ran.</span><br /><br /><span>This work has changed my life. It taught me how to listen, lead, compromise, and stay true to my values. I am excited for what the final five months of my term will bring. I will keep showing up and finish this chapter with the same drive I had on day one.</span><br /><br /><span>As this part of my life comes to a close, I am also looking forward to the chapter and what it holds. I am still putting the pieces together of what that will exactly look like, but I will carry forward everything this experience has given me.</span><br /><br /><span>To everyone who helped me along the way: thank you. There are too many to name, but you know who you are. You offered advice, encouragement, honest feedback, and support when it mattered most. I will never forget that. You have a special place in my heart.</span><br /><br /><span>I do not believe in career politicians; I believe in career public service. No one is meant to hold power forever. I did not run because I thought I was the best person to represent South Windsor. I ran because I believed it was time for a change. I believe it is time again.</span><br /><br /><span>South Windsor is ready for a new voice.</span><br /><br /><span>Thank you for trusting me.</span><br /><br /><span>Your neighbor,</span><br /><span>Alan Cavagnaro</span></div>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Testimony in Support of House Bill 5004]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/testimony-in-support-of-house-bill-5004]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/testimony-in-support-of-house-bill-5004#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Mon, 03 Mar 2025 20:35:08 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/testimony-in-support-of-house-bill-5004</guid><description><![CDATA[       H.B. 5004 - An Act Concerning the Protection of the Environment and the Development&nbsp;of Renewable Energy Sources and Associated Job Sectors&nbsp;Testimony of Alan CavagnaroMarch 3, 2025&nbsp;Dear Co-Chairs Lopes and Parker, Ranking Members Harding and Callahan, and members ofthe Environment Committee,&nbsp;&#8203;My name is Alan Cavagnaro. I am a student at the University of Connecticut, a Planning and Zoning Commissioner from South Windsor, and the Deputy Director of DesegregateCT. T [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/screenshot-20250303-162805-youtube_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%"></div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><span><span style="font-weight:700">H.B. 5004 - An Act Concerning the Protection of the Environment and the Development&nbsp;</span></span><br /><span><span style="font-weight:700">of Renewable Energy Sources and Associated Job Sectors&nbsp;<br /></span></span><span style="font-weight:700">Testimony of Alan Cavagnaro</span><span><span style="font-weight:700"><br /></span></span><span style="font-weight:700">March 3, 2025&nbsp;</span><span><span style="font-weight:700"></span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Dear Co-Chairs Lopes and Parker, Ranking Members Harding and Callahan, and members of</span></span><br /><span><span>the Environment Committee,&nbsp;<br /><br />&#8203;</span></span><span>My name is Alan Cavagnaro. I am a student at the University of Connecticut, a Planning and Zoning Commissioner from South Windsor, and the Deputy Director of DesegregateCT. Tonight, I am testifying on my own behalf, representing myself. I am in full support of <a href="https://www.cga.ct.gov/2025/TOB/H/PDF/2025HB-05004-R01-HB.PDF" target="_blank">House Bill 5004</a>.</span><br /><br /><span><span>Climate change is a real and urgent threat that affects everyone, including future generations. House Bill 5004 addresses this by incentivizing clean energy projects and setting new greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions standards, intending to achieve net-zero emissions by 2050. This bill also takes steps to modernize our energy infrastructure in ways that are both cost-effective and environmentally responsible.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>The future of Connecticut&rsquo;s energy system must prioritize efficiency, affordability, and sustainability. H.B. 5004 includes provisions that will lower consumer costs by advancing energy-efficient appliances, expanding clean heating solutions like heat pumps, and investing in state buildings that generate their own electricity through solar. Additionally, supporting solar canopies over large parking lots and the development of thermal energy networks will make clean energy more accessible while reducing strain on the grid.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>I strongly support the provisions in H.B. 5004 that advance Connecticut&rsquo;s decarbonization efforts, particularly through renewable energy expansion and electrification. The transition away from natural gas toward clean energy sources such as solar and wind is essential not only for meeting our updated GHG reduction targets but also for ensuring energy security and economic stability.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Connecticut already faces some of the highest electricity rates in the country, and continued reliance on natural gas, especially given supply volatility and aging infrastructure, only exacerbates this burden on ratepayers. Investing in solar energy and battery storage will help stabilize long-term energy costs while reducing harmful emissions and creating good-paying jobs in the clean energy sector. As municipalities play a key role in permitting and land use decisions for renewable energy projects, we must align local zoning policies with the state&rsquo;s broader decarbonization goals to accelerate the deployment of clean energy infrastructure.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>On a more personal note, I recently returned from Baku, Azerbaijan, where I attended the United Nations Climate Change Conference (COP29) alongside other students as part of UConn&rsquo;s fellowship program. There, I saw firsthand why climate action is imperative for change. During this time, I met local residents of Baku who shared their concerns about the incoming Trump administration and the potential policies and decisions that could undermine global trust in our collective efforts for a sound and sustainable future.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Leaders from around the world are taking steps to reduce emissions, invest in clean energy, and prepare for the realities of a warming planet. Connecticut must do the same. We as a country cannot afford to lag behind when the consequences of inaction, such as rising temperatures, extreme weather, and economic instability, are already impacting our communities.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Beyond environmental benefits, these measures will create jobs in emerging energy sectors and help our communities adapt to the realities of climate change. Connecticut has a unique opportunity to lead on these issues, setting an example for others. Passing House Bill 5004 is a critical step in building a better tomorrow.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Thank you for considering my testimony.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Sincerely,</span></span><br /><span><span>Alan Cavagnaro</span></span><br /><br /><em>This written testimony was submitted to the Connecticut General Assembly's Joint Environment Committee for the public hearing on March 3, 2025. Testimony from that hearing can be found&nbsp;<a href="https://www.cga.ct.gov/aspx/CGADisplayTestimonies/CGADisplayTestimony.aspx?bill=HB-05004&amp;doc_year=2025" target="_blank">here</a>.</em></div>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Testimony in Support of House Bill 6831]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/testimony-in-support-of-work-live-ride-hb-6831]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/testimony-in-support-of-work-live-ride-hb-6831#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Tue, 04 Feb 2025 21:17:51 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/testimony-in-support-of-work-live-ride-hb-6831</guid><description><![CDATA[       HB 6831 - An Act Concerning Transit-Oriented Communities&nbsp;Alan Cavagnaro, Deputy Director, DesegregateCTFebruary 3, 2025Chairwoman Kavros-DeGraw, Chairman Rahman, Vice Chairman Needleman, Vice Chairman Chafee, Ranking Member Zullo, Ranking Member Gordon, and Distinguished Members of the Connecticut General Assembly Joint Planning and Development Committee:My name is Alan Cavagnaro. I am the Deputy Director for DesegregateCT, a student at the University of Connecticut, a Planning and Z [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/1000032566_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%"></div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph" style="text-align:left;"><span><span style="font-weight:700">HB 6831 - An Act Concerning Transit-Oriented Communities&nbsp;</span></span><br /><span><span style="font-weight:700">Alan Cavagnaro, Deputy Director, DesegregateCT</span></span><br /><span><span style="font-weight:700">February 3, 2025</span></span><br /><br /><span><span style="color:rgb(29, 28, 29)">Chairwoman Kavros-DeGraw, Chairman Rahman, Vice Chairman Needleman, Vice Chairman Chafee, Ranking Member Zullo, Ranking Member Gordon, and Distinguished Members of the Connecticut General Assembly Joint Planning and Development Committee:</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>My name is Alan Cavagnaro. I am the Deputy Director for DesegregateCT, a student at the University of Connecticut, a Planning and Zoning Commissioner from South Windsor, and the Vice Chair of the Housing and Fair Rent Commission from South Windsor. Tonight, I am testifying on behalf of myself.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span style="font-weight:700">Economic Impact of Work Live Ride</span></span><br /><span><span>I would like to start by discussing why Work Live Ride is an economic success for municipalities. For far too long, our communities have relied on stagnant growth in development to contribute to the tax base. The outdated approach of requiring large lots and stringent development regulations has proven unsustainable for economic growth. This has created a ripple effect that hinders municipalities from reducing taxes and seeing a positive return on investment.</span></span><br /><br /><span>Restrictive local zoning policies limit housing supply and create significant economic barriers in high-productivity areas. Research from Chang-Tai Hsieh (University of Chicago, Booth School of Business), Enrico Moretti (University of California, Berkeley, Department of Economics), and the National Bureau of Economic Research (NBER) reveals that these constraints led to a 50% reduction in aggregate U.S. growth from 1964 to 2009, as too many workers were unable to access economic opportunities (<a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w21154/w21154.pdf" target="_blank">NBER, May 2015, Revised May 2017</a>).</span><br /><br /><span><span style="font-weight:700">Importance of TOCs in Connecticut</span></span><br /><span><span>Transit-Oriented Communities (TOCs) offer a unique solution because they provide both density and accessibility, key elements for economic growth. By integrating housing with transportation infrastructure, we create environments where businesses, workers, and residents can thrive in close proximity. These communities stimulate local economies, reduce commuting costs, and foster vibrant areas where people want to live, work, and visit. As we continue to expand housing options around transit hubs, TOCs can serve as engines for both economic and social progress in Connecticut.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Work Live Ride is a middle-ground solution to work with municipalities to create more homes. It is part of the &ldquo;all of the above&rdquo; approach to solving Connecticut's housing crisis. Local commissions deserve the final say on the size and placement of TOC districts. Allowing communities to opt-in to Work Live Ride ensures that each town can tailor solutions to its unique needs.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span style="font-weight:700">Student Impact of Work Live Ride</span></span><br /><span><span>As a commuter student at UConn, I rely on my car due to the financial burden of limited on-campus housing, rising higher education costs, and the lack of sufficient public transportation options to Storrs. While I am fortunate to have this option, many of my classmates do not. Car dependency presents significant barriers to academic success. By embracing Transit-Oriented Communities, we can create more accessible, connected spaces that reduce the challenges associated with car-dependent sprawl.</span></span><br /><br /><span>Research by Natalie Butler and Francis Torres of the <a href="https://bipartisanpolicy.org/blog/housing-insecurity-and-homelessness-among-college-students/" target="_blank">Bipartisan Policy Center</a>&nbsp;indicates that nearly 48% of university students experience housing insecurity, underscoring the urgent need for more accessible housing. In Connecticut, expanding housing near transit hubs would offer young people better access to education, jobs, and essential services while reducing the reliance on cars.</span><br /><br /><span><span>Work Live Ride will allow all 169 municipalities to explore new means of development and sustainable policy near existing transit. I ask for your support of HB 6831. Thank you for your time and consideration.</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Respectfully,</span></span><br /><br /><span><span>Alan Cavagnaro</span></span><br /><span><span>Deputy Director, DesegregateCT</span></span><br /><br /><em>This written testimony was submitted to the Connecticut General Assembly's Joint Planning and Development Committee for the public hearing on February 3, 2025. Testimony from that hearing can be found <a href="https://www.cga.ct.gov/aspx/CGADisplayTestimonies/CGADisplayTestimony.aspx?bill=HB-06831&amp;doc_year=2025" target="_blank">here</a>.</em></div>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[From Local Zoning to Global Climate Policy: Lessons in Storytelling and Urbanism at COP29 (2024 Repost)]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/from-local-zoning-to-global-climate-policy-lessons-in-storytelling-and-urbanism-at-cop29-repost]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/from-local-zoning-to-global-climate-policy-lessons-in-storytelling-and-urbanism-at-cop29-repost#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Wed, 04 Dec 2024 00:05:21 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/from-local-zoning-to-global-climate-policy-lessons-in-storytelling-and-urbanism-at-cop29-repost</guid><description><![CDATA[ 	 		 			 				 					 						            					 								 					 						  &#8203;As a local zoning commissioner, I have spent countless hours shaping housing policies and open space plans &mdash; but I never imagined how closely these efforts would align with what I experienced at COP29 in Azerbaijan. I had the opportunity to observe throughout week two, mainly in the Blue Zone, official proceedings that would shape the next decade of global financial obligations for climate change. Although worlds ap [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div><div class="wsite-multicol"><div class="wsite-multicol-table-wrap" style="margin:0 -15px;"> 	<table class="wsite-multicol-table"> 		<tbody class="wsite-multicol-tbody"> 			<tr class="wsite-multicol-tr"> 				<td class="wsite-multicol-col" style="width:50%; padding:0 15px;"> 					 						  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/54181191231-ba05834188-o_orig.png" alt="View: Baku, Azerbaijan" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%"></div> </div></div>  <div class="wsite-spacer" style="height:24px;"></div>   					 				</td>				<td class="wsite-multicol-col" style="width:50%; padding:0 15px;"> 					 						  <div class="paragraph">&#8203;<span style="color:rgb(0, 14, 47)">As a local zoning commissioner, I have spent countless hours shaping housing policies and open space plans &mdash; but I never imagined how closely these efforts would align with what I experienced at COP29 in Azerbaijan. I had the opportunity to observe throughout week two, mainly in the Blue Zone, official proceedings that would shape the next decade of global financial obligations for climate change. Although worlds apart in scope, sitting in the negotiation room as an observer to amend articles on my final day in the Blue Zone felt surprisingly familiar. It reminded me of my work back home in South Windsor, where I often debate and advocate for changes to language on my own commission.</span></div>   					 				</td>			</tr> 		</tbody> 	</table> </div></div></div>  <div class="paragraph">&#8203;<span style="color:rgb(0, 14, 47)">During this journey, I found myself constantly drawing comparisons to home. Before this trip, I had never traveled outside the country &mdash; or even been on a plane &mdash; until I left the States for a week for my flight to Azerbaijan. The mountains, the city, the infrastructure &mdash; I found myself comparing them to what I knew, wondering why they looked the way they did. I was struck by how clean Baku was as a city, compared to what I knew in the United States. I admired the thoughtful infrastructure dedicated to pedestrian safety, the accessible and plentiful modes of transportation, and how they had preserved the old part of the city for tourism &mdash; something we often fail to do in the United States. I only encountered friendly faces on my travels, and when I interacted with locals, they would always ask me what the United States was like and if it was as dangerous as many make it out to be. At that moment, it was a wake-up call. It gave me a new sense of urbanism that we at home have failed to protect our very own.</span></div>  <span class='imgPusher' style='float:right;height:0px'></span><span style='display: table;width:auto;position:relative;float:right;max-width:100%;;clear:right;margin-top:0px;*margin-top:0px'><a><img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/54181630425-192dcd1426-o_orig.png" style="margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-left: 0px; margin-right: 10px; border-width:0; max-width:100%" alt="Picture" class="galleryImageBorder wsite-image" /></a><span style="display: table-caption; caption-side: bottom; font-size: 90%; margin-top: -10px; margin-bottom: 10px; text-align: center;" class="wsite-caption"></span></span> <div class="paragraph" style="text-align:left;display:block;">&#8203;<span style="color:rgb(0, 14, 47)">One of the most powerful lessons I have taken away from COP29 is the art of storytelling as a tool to advance policy. While data and facts are essential, something I often focus on, they frequently fail to connect on an emotional level. People support what they can relate to, and storytelling bridges that gap, turning abstract policies into palatable realities. An example I have come up with is rather than sharing a percentage or statistic supporting any argument to incorporate roundabouts in a suburban community, create a story of what could happen. Talk about feeling of safety and the potential impact for school children when increasing safety on our roads. That argument alone has the potential to win over parents, and most likely, the majority of the room. During my time at COP29, I observed how presenters used narratives to humanize complex challenges and inspire action. Whether it was a story about a community transformed by active transport or a personal anecdote highlighting the real-world impact of climate policies, these stories brought the issues to life. It became clear to me that storytelling is not just a skill but a necessity for effective leadership.</span></div> <hr style="width:100%;clear:both;visibility:hidden;"></hr>  <div class="paragraph"><span style="color:rgb(0, 14, 47)">As of writing this, I know I am nearing the end of a chapter in my life as a student at UConn. Truthfully, I do not know what the next page looks like. But I do know the changes I want to carry forward with me. If I am truly committed to making an impact, both at home and globally, I must become more open to the many ways I can drive change. This was a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity to speak first-hand with leaders in the space. It would be foolish to squander advice that comes so rarely. There are only so many seconds in life to embrace and learn from these moments, and I hope to make the most of them.<br /><br /></span><em style="color:rgb(0, 14, 47)">Alan Cavagnaro is a Political Science and Urban and Community Studies major in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences." -&nbsp;<a href="https://sustainability.uconn.edu/2024/12/03/from-local-zoning-to-global-climate-policy-lessons-in-storytelling-and-urbanism-at-cop29-alan-cavagnaro/" target="_blank">UConn Office of Sustainability, 2024.</a></em><span style="color:rgb(0, 14, 47)"></span><br /></div>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[COP29: Challenges, Opportunities, and the Promise of Global Action: My Perspective (2024 Repost)]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/cop29-challenges-opportunities-and-the-promise-of-global-action-my-perspective-alan-cavagnaro-2024-repost]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/cop29-challenges-opportunities-and-the-promise-of-global-action-my-perspective-alan-cavagnaro-2024-repost#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Thu, 21 Nov 2024 06:19:33 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/cop29-challenges-opportunities-and-the-promise-of-global-action-my-perspective-alan-cavagnaro-2024-repost</guid><description><![CDATA[       &#8203;"This year, the 2024 United Nations Climate Change Conference (COP29) has the power to do something exceptional. With stakeholders, negotiators, and observers from across the world in attendance (including myself), this should be a special moment to focus on the issues that matter most. COP29 has been referred to as the &ldquo;finance COP&rdquo; due to its significance in readdressing the financial obligations of all parties involved. While this event occurs annually, this conferen [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/1000027417_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%"></div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph" style="text-align:left;"><span>&#8203;"This year, the 2024 United Nations Climate Change Conference (COP29) has the power to do something exceptional. With stakeholders, negotiators, and observers from across the world in attendance (including myself), this should be a special moment to focus on the issues that matter most. COP29 has been referred to as the &ldquo;finance COP&rdquo; due to its significance in readdressing the financial obligations of all parties involved. While this event occurs annually, this conference specifically plays a critical role in geopolitical financial responsibilities for years to come.<br /><br />With this in mind, I journeyed through the Blue Zone at Baku Stadium in Azerbaijan, the host location for this year&rsquo;s conference. This event is traditionally split between two sections, both the Green and Blue Zones. As of this writing, I have only explored the Blue Zone, which serves as the venue for formal negotiations, panels, and pavilion events. The Green Zone, from what I have heard, offers a more interactive experience with activity hubs and side panels featuring various stakeholders.</span><br />&#8203;<br /><span>My week began on Monday, November 18, observing official proceedings in the Plenary Hall. The most impactful moment of COP29 so far came during the very first event I attended.&nbsp;</span><span style="font-weight:400"><a href="https://www.fbcnews.com.fj/news/calls-for-urgent-action-at-cop29/" target="_blank">Attorney General Graham Leung of Fiji</a></span><span>&nbsp;delivered a passionate address, stating:&nbsp;</span><em>&ldquo;On finance ambition, let me emphasize again that an ambitious outcome must focus not only on how much but also on how the finance will flow.&rdquo;</em><span>&nbsp;(FBC News)&nbsp; His concern highlighted a pressing issue: many parties to existing global climate agreements, such as the Paris Climate Agreement, have yet to pay their fair share of the commitment. Even among those who have contributed, questions remain about whether these contributions are leading in the correct direction &mdash; both in terms of communities and industries. Leung called for strong financial provisions and a minimum threshold to ensure fair and effective resource allocation for loss, damage, and adaptation efforts.&nbsp;</span><br /><br /><span>It is undeniably true that some countries have sought to exploit the principles of the Paris Climate Agreement, including their Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs). During a Sustainable Transport panel at the Canadian Pavilion, it was revealed that certain nations have focused on building more roads to score points toward meeting their NDCs, while neglecting critical investments in public transportation infrastructure.</span><br /><br /><span>This was the prevailing theme on my first day &mdash; parties voicing dissatisfaction with the efforts of others. Informally, Monday seemed dominated by a &ldquo;blame game&rdquo; atmosphere. This was no surprise as world leaders spent their days during the world summit last week,&nbsp;</span><span style="font-weight:400"><a href="https://www.politico.eu/article/azerbaijan-baku-president-ilham-aliyev-fossil-fuel-western-hypocrisy-cop-29-opener/">antagonizing and throwing blame</a> <a href="https://www.politico.eu/article/azerbaijan-baku-president-ilham-aliyev-fossil-fuel-western-hypocrisy-cop-29-opener/">across the world stage</a></span><span>&nbsp;of who was responsible rather than offering solutions. What should also not go unnoticed is the fact that many countries who are here are the ones accountable for the mess that we find ourselves in.<br /><br />Major oil-producing countries, such as Qatar, Saudi Arabia, Oman, the United Arab Emirates, Kuwait, etc, all have a larger-than-expected presence at this summit. Most have glamorous and extravagant pavilion locations, dishing out free food to all who would like, attempting to greenwash their history when it comes to oil production. Let it be known that we are not perfect either. Both the United States as well as China face their own hypocrisy on the world stage, championing climate solutions while being among the biggest contributors to the problem. To objectively view these talks, it is essential to approach them with an awareness of this underlying hypocrisy and complexity.</span><br /><br /><span>COP29 has not been as grim as I initially made it out to be. On Tuesday, November 19, and Wednesday, November 20, I drew on my first-day experiences to better prepare for reentering the Blue Zone. I knew that I actively enjoyed the pavilions as parties were tasked with managing their own panels. Building on that understanding, I attended the&nbsp;</span><span><a href="https://www.youtube.com/live/zT3Egi1ctuw?si=XJFWkBXAsPNWiL5W" target="_blank">British Pavilion event on Active Transport</a></span><span>. One striking fact from the panel was that every dollar invested in public transportation yields a $5 return on investment. The discussion focused on strategies to promote sustainable transit initiatives to local communities. During the event, I had the opportunity to meet British Member of Parliament (MP) Mary Creagh who was also the Parliamentary Under-Secretary of State for Nature. She gave me some of the most important advice I could have received at this point in my life. As a Planning and Zoning Commissioner in my hometown of South Windsor, CT, promoting urbanist principles can sometimes be a challenging endeavor. I asked her for insights on achieving breakthroughs, and she emphasized the importance of empathetic leadership and storytelling to persuade stakeholders and drive progress. Her guidance could not have come at a better time in my journey.<br />&#8203;</span><br /><span>If you were to ask me for my takeaway from COP29 so far with what remains, I would say to be critical but ambitious about what is possible. The majority of attendees understand the setting we are all in. Every COP cannot be a landmark oasis of agreements and principles. Yet, each conference holds the potential to lay crucial groundwork, build momentum, and inspire the bold actions needed to address our shared climate crisis.</span><br /><br /><em style="color:rgb(0, 14, 47)">Alan Cavagnaro is a Political Science and Urban and Community Studies major in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences." -&nbsp;<a href="https://sustainability.uconn.edu/2024/11/20/cop29-challenges-opportunities-and-the-promise-of-global-action-my-perspective-alan-cavagnaro/" target="_blank">UConn Office of Sustainability, 2024.</a></em></div>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Opinion: Creating opportunities for youth: The promise of Work, Live, Ride (2023 Repost)]]></title><link><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/opinion-creating-opportunities-for-youth-the-promise-of-work-live-ride-2023-repost]]></link><comments><![CDATA[https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/opinion-creating-opportunities-for-youth-the-promise-of-work-live-ride-2023-repost#comments]]></comments><pubDate>Mon, 28 Oct 2024 21:25:52 GMT</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/opinion-creating-opportunities-for-youth-the-promise-of-work-live-ride-2023-repost</guid><description><![CDATA[       "If, like many of my friends, you&rsquo;re graduating from university and getting ready to tackle the professional world, you&rsquo;ll need some luck finding a home in Connecticut. This problem is not exclusive to Connecticut but one that we, as a state, have&nbsp;struggled to solve&nbsp;.&#8203;This dilemma is why I got involved in the pro-homes movement, advocating for local and state zoning reforms that work for everyone. I&rsquo;m still in college, living at home, but will face this p [...] ]]></description><content:encoded><![CDATA[<div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0;margin-right:0;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="https://www.alancavagnaro.com/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/work-live-ride-illo_orig.png" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%"></div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph" style="text-align:left;">"If, like many of my friends, you&rsquo;re graduating from university and getting ready to tackle the professional world, you&rsquo;ll need some luck finding a home in Connecticut. This problem is not exclusive to Connecticut but one that we, as a state, have&nbsp;<a href="https://www.chfa.org/assets/1/6/Connecticut_HNA.pdf">struggled to solve&nbsp;</a>.<br /><br />&#8203;This dilemma is why I got involved in the pro-homes movement, advocating for local and state zoning reforms that work for everyone. I&rsquo;m still in college, living at home, but will face this problem soon. As a lifelong Nutmegger, there is a part of me that wants to stay in Connecticut and start my career, but I know that may not be practical. For many of my generation,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/personalfinance/real-estate/2023/02/24/genz-moving-big-cities-migration/11337814002/">it isn&rsquo;t</a>.<br /><br /><span style="color:rgb(17, 17, 17)">The housing crisis impacts everyone in our state. Whether it&rsquo;s our failure to retain skillful young individuals, our incapability to provide affordable homes to those who need them, or our failure to build homes in sustainable places, we must do better. This is why I&rsquo;m fighting for change by supporting&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.cga.ct.gov/asp/cgabillstatus/cgabillstatus.asp?selBillType=Bill&amp;bill_num=HB6890&amp;which_year=2023" target="_blank">H.B. 6890</a><span style="color:rgb(17, 17, 17)">, also known as &ldquo;Work Live Ride.&rdquo;<br /><br />Work Live Ride helps towns and cities establish Transit-Oriented Community (TOC) districts that promote the construction of new homes and businesses near bus or rail stations with state funding and support provided for towns that opt-in. In particular, it incentivizes &ldquo;</span><a href="https://missingmiddlehousing.com/">missing middle</a><span style="color:rgb(17, 17, 17)">&nbsp;homes and requires a certain percentage of homes to be affordable through the Connecticut Housing Finance Authority (CHFA)&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.chfa.org/about-us/ct-opportunity-map/">opportunity map</a><span style="color:rgb(17, 17, 17)">.<br /><br />The &ldquo;Work&rdquo; part of this bill details how we can build multi-family, mixed-use homes and businesses to retain our younger population, like myself. Look at all the empty buildings located on or near car-centric plazas that are not within walking distance from any home. That&rsquo;s why they&rsquo;re vacant; these places see little foot traffic. Nobody will explore a new business if you drive by it from afar. We need more walkable mixed-use developments that put people within walking distance of jobs and small businesses rather than car-dependent sprawl that kills economic vitality.<br /><br />The next part of the bill is &ldquo;Live.&rdquo; Many homeowners in Connecticut prefer to maintain single-family zoning and uphold minimum lot size requirements that can result in higher living costs for prospective homeowners. When starting out, most young people are looking for smaller homes, like an apartment, not a big single-family house requiring two acres. Young people also want to&nbsp;</span><a href="https://insidesources.com/walkable-cities-are-where-people-want-to-live-and-spend/" target="_blank">walk more and drive less,&nbsp;</a><span style="color:rgb(17, 17, 17)">even in the suburbs, but we don&rsquo;t zone for it. This is what causes many young people (including some of my friends) to leave the state and look for new communities to find a home.<br /><br />The last critical part is &ldquo;Ride.&rdquo; These TOC districts will be located close to public transit stations. Work Live Ride will help increase ridership of our bus and rail systems, making it easier for people to get around without a car and making more of a case for expanding transit service. This will reduce traffic congestion, air pollution, and carbon emissions while preserving our forests and farms from development. My generation will be living through climate change; fighting it is important to us, and this bill does.<br /><br />Work Live Ride poses as a first step to solving the overarching consequence of our inability to zone for a growing population and a changing economy and climate. There is a saying I&rsquo;ve heard that town planners use: &ldquo;you zone for what you want.&rdquo; Many&nbsp;</span><a href="https://homebase.ai/smart-apartments/millennials-city-life-to-suburbs/">young people want mixed-use, multi-family development</a><span style="color:rgb(17, 17, 17)">, but we aren&rsquo;t represented in these decisions. I&rsquo;m trying to change that, and I know Work Live Ride will help in those efforts so that our communities work for everyone.</span><br /><br /><em style="color:rgb(17, 17, 17)">Alan Cavagnaro is Campaign Coordinator for&nbsp;<a href="https://www.desegregatect.org/" target="_blank">DesegregateCT.</a>" - <a href="https://ctmirror.org/2023/06/01/ct-planning-zoning-reform-bill-work-live-ride/" target="_blank">CT Mirror, 2023</a>.</em><br /><br /></div>]]></content:encoded></item></channel></rss>